the topic of Taxation comes up on the  program a lot and the reason it comes up  is because taxes are both a political  issue and an economic issue and a social  issue when it comes to what our  society's priorities via taxation and  who we want to be taxing so just as like  some examples for you we've talked about  what is the tax rate that maximizes  revenue for the government and who  should be taxed at what rate in order to  obtain the maximum amount of revenue  before tax has become a disincentive to  productivity how do certain tax plans  impact people on the basis of the amount  of money that they make have tax cuts  and tax increases historically help the  economy or hurt the economy and how do  we measure it is it better for the  economy to cut or raise taxes on the  rich what about on the poor what about  the middle class supply side versus  demand side we've talked about all of  those things before and I'm not going to  talk about any of those things today  okay what I'm going to focus in on today  is the legal basis for taxation in the  United States not generally the legal  basis for taxation around the world or  as a concept but in the United States  what is the legal basis for taxation now  you might wonder if you're a reasonable  person why the hell are you going to  talk about that obviously it's legal for  the government to tax people well it  might be obvious to you but there are  people to whom it is not obvious this  relates to the group that says taxation  is theft or the government is stealing  or even that taxation is actually  illegal and it's good to know exactly  how to respond because the real  conversations we should be having are  the ones we've been having on the show  that I mentioned two minutes ago but to  get to that point you need to get past  the objections that some people have  which are that taxes are just  straight-up illegal so first of all I  can't refute every version of the  there's no law that says you have to pay  taxes argument because there are dozens  and dozens of versions of that argument  but I'm going to lay out a general free  work for you that will at least give you  a starting point to talk to people when  they make these assertions so we'll  start with the legal portion then we'll  talk about the ideological portion which  is more the idea of taxes being theft  the general right to collect taxes is in  the Constitution specifically it's in  the sixteenth Amendment of the Bill of  Rights and it says quote the Congress  shall have power to lay and collect  taxes on incomes from whatever source  derived without apportionment among the  several states and without regard to any  census or enumeration the key word there  is incomes what counts as taxable  incomes well for information on that we  go to the united states code of law 26  subsection 63 which says that taxable  income is simply gross income - allowed  deductions tax law enumerates what  deductions are available to people this  includes stuff like the mortgage  interest deduction or loss carryovers  all of these different things that we  talk about on a year-to-year basis and  that's how your income is defined so  that gets us to you must pay taxes all  right that is in the Constitution and  that's in the United States code of law  but some people will argue where does  the law say that you have to file a tax  return every year what about that  element well we also go to the United  States code of law for that United  States code 26 subsection 6 0 1 2 which  requires the filing of tax returns by  every individual who has gross income  for the year and there are dozens and  dozens of legal cases that have upheld  this so now I know that this is so  general that there will be anti-tax  people who will write to me and they'll  say David you know what you're ignoring  when you tell us about this are the  technicalities or the semantics or  particular wordings that they assert  prove taxation is illegal but the bottom  line is as a general framework when  people say there's no  law that says the government can collect  taxes the sixteenth Amendment plus the  United States code of law plus dozens of  cases worth of legal precedent laid out  very very clearly constitutional law  scholars pay their taxes because they  know that it's legal  accountants pay their taxes because they  know that they have to economists pay  their taxes taxation is legal so let's  now move on to the sort of more  ideological political argument that  taxes are theft okay the main argument  is that taxation is theft because you  don't have a choice about paying taxes  if you don't pay your taxes men with  guns as they like to say will show up  and put you in jail or prison it's an  initiation of force and therefore  taxation is theft in the ideological  political sense but number one men with  guns is an argument for improper  initiations of force is silly in and of  itself I don't want to focus on that  piece today because I feel like it's not  really central to what we're really  trying to figure out here there is a  social contract which includes taxation  enforcing taxation by the government is  merely enforcing that contract now  anti-tax libertarians will say I never  signed that contract how can I be party  to a contract that I never signed or  agreed to participate in well actually  you did okay one possibility is that  your parents chose for you to live in  the United States after you were born  they have power of custody that's the  law and you therefore are part of the  social contract that is the United  States of America because of the  decision your parents made when they had  custody of you and you can leave at any  time if you can find another country  that will have you that relates to the  law of other countries not to the United  States  if you immigrated to the United States  you signed the contract called the USA  when you took your oath of citizenship  you swore to uphold our laws which  include taxation as described into the  Constitution sixteenth Amendment and the  United States called  code of law if you're not a citizen but  you're here as a permanent resident or  on a visa or with a green card you did  the exact same thing as part of  receiving that apparatus which allows  you to be in the United States legally  the fact that you may not have  physically signed it is really  meaningless if you go to a restaurant  and order food would it be valid to say  I don't really owe the restaurant any  money because I didn't sign a contract  no that would be a really dumb argument  it would be a childish libertarian  argument and that's exactly the argument  that they are making when they say that  taxation is theft now let's go to the  next part of their argument which is  that you don't have the option not to  pay taxes to the American government  that's also not true and it really comes  down to what do you mean by options if  you truly don't want to pay taxes to the  United States government for whatever  reason you have the option of not  working that's your call how you survive  would be up to you to figure out in the  libertarian paradise but it is your call  if you don't want to pay taxes one way  is not to have any income number two you  can leave the country now as I mentioned  you would then be subject to the laws of  other countries in terms of whether you  could even live and work in those  countries and by the way other countries  also have taxes so that would be sort of  a practical limitation you can also  lobby to have the law changed so that  there are no more taxes now I think it  would be difficult to do that because  most people understand that we need  taxes humans not in a state of nature  working in groups that are bigger than a  hundred and fifty people to pick a sort  of evolutionary relevant number need  taxes there's simply no other way to  carry out a lot of these basic  mechanisms for human life so I think it  would be difficult to go ahead and  convince enough of the population that  we don't need taxes but you could also  Lobby to have the law change you could  lobby your elected officials or whoever  else and then number four you could also  just take a chance on not paying your  taxes and see if you get caught maybe  you wouldn't that would certainly be a  choice the  you would have to make so taxes are not  illegal the sixteenth amendment the  united states code of law lay all of  that out and taxes are also not theft  you have a number of different options  if you don't want to pay taxes to the  United States of America  this is relevant shockingly because  there is a surprisingly high portion of  libertarians that believe this now I  know that lots of sane libertarians will  say I'm really more of a social  libertarian I I'm more of a  constitutional libertarian or they will  apply civil libertarian other words to  it but there are libertarians that  believe this stuff and you have to get  past this in order to get to what I  consider to be the really important  questions about taxation meaning what  should be the priorities of taxation who  should be paying taxes how much should  they be paying and how should that money  be spent and what the society most  benefit benefit from the usage of those  taxes on if I'm missing something if you  think that the legal framework that I've  laid out is not sufficient to defend  taxation as a legal mechanism I want to  hear from you I'm on Twitter at Dee  Pakman and the show is on Twitter at  David Pakman show let me know and I will  be more than willing to have my mind  changed if you make a coherent and  compelling argument
 

